# Group a expansion scenes: N01-N10, N41, N42, P01, P02, P05, P09 (QW1, QW6, QC17 surface half, QC14 first beat).
# ================================================================ N01 Hollins Mill (R01 village, CH01)
@scene N01_HOLLIN
if phase:post post
say hollin | Forty years I've ground Crown grain. The Crown weighs the flour. I weigh what's left.
say hollin | What's left is small. Don't let the size of the wheel fool you.
choice | Ask about the village -> village | Ask about the roads -> road | Leave -> bye
label village
say hollin | Six families, one wheel, one pond. My granddaughter Nell runs the stones now. I run my mouth.
say hollin | Juniper at the store mends anything with a hinge. Bring her scraps and she'll make you something honest.
end
label road
say hollin | North, the Oathstone Crossroads. There's a peddler at the milestone who sells what the Crown forgot to tax.
say hollin | East, past Wren's orchard, the old chapel sank into the marsh. Its bells still ring some nights. Nobody's pulling them.
rumor | Hollins Mill: the Sunken Chapel's bells ring on their own, in the marsh east of Wren's Orchard.
end
label post
say hollin | The river moved in the night. Picked itself up and went two fields east. The wheel turns anyway.
say hollin | We grind for anybody now. There's nobody left to weigh it.
label bye
@end

@scene N01_MILLER
if phase:post post
say brannock | Two sacks up, one sack down. The one going down is ours. The Crown carts the rest to Veyr.
say brannock | I used to carry for the quarry. At least grain doesn't fall on you.
end
label post
say brannock | Refugees from Veyr sleep in the loft. They grind their own and sing at the stones. Off-key. It's nice.
@end

@scene N01_APPRENTICE
say apprentice | Old Hollin's mother kept a garden over the race, across the plank bridge. She died, he stopped going.
say apprentice | He says she buried her good baking gloves out there so the assessors couldn't list them. Nobody's looked.
@end

@scene N01_FISHER
if flag:n01_oda post
set n01_oda
say oda | New faces. Here's how the pond works: stand at the end of the dock, face the water, and wait.
say oda | Don't jerk the line when it twitches. That's the pond testing you. When it pulls, you pull.
say oda | Fish bite best at dusk. Here - my spare bait and a snack for the wait. Pay me back in stories.
give I001 2
end
label post
if phase:post ruin
say oda | The carp in there are older than the assessor and twice as polite. The dock end's yours whenever you like.
end
label ruin
say oda | The pond's the only water that didn't move when the ground broke. I think the carp held it down.
@end

@scene N01_FISH
if flag:n01_fished done
set n01_fished
say narr | You drop a line off the dock end. The float sits. The float thinks about it.
say narr | Then it goes under, hard. Something heavy and whiskered comes up, looks at you, and spits out a coin-sized tonic flask.
give I001 1
say narr | Oda, from the shore: 'He does that. Somebody drowned a crate of those in the flood of '06.'
end
label done
say narr | The float sits on the brown water. The old carp is somewhere below, thinking about you.
@end

@scene N01_WHEEL
say narr | The paddles are patched with three colours of Crown crate. One board still reads 'PROPERTY OF THE MINISTRY - DO NOT'.
@end

@scene N01_CHILD
if phase:post post
say child | I'm chasing the ducks! The assessor counts them every month, so I count them first. There's nine. He writes eight.
end
label post
say child | The ducks came back! Nobody counts them now. I still do. Eleven.
@end

@scene N01_FARMER
say worker | Barley. The Crown takes the good heads and leaves the chaff for the bread. We call it 'Crown loaf'. Not fondly.
say worker | Hounds come down off the toll road at night after the rats in the stook. Keep to the lane after dark.
@end

@scene N01_ASSESSOR
say assessor | Grain assessor, Ministry of Provision. Please don't stand between me and the scales.
say assessor | Everything here is counted: sacks, ducks, hinges. It's not personal. It's just everything.
@end

@scene N01_LAUNDRESS
say baker | My husband went to work the toll at Gallowgate. They pay in chits, and the chits are only good at the toll canteen.
say baker | They've a bounty board there now. Hounds, bandits, 'persons of interest'. Half the names on it are ours.
rumor | Hollins Mill: Gallowgate Toll keeps a bounty board. Some of the names on it are villagers.
@end

@scene N01_WIDOW
say elder | My grandmother used to say a king was buried twice out west, under the ash barrows. Once in a crown, once without.
say elder | Children dare each other to find the door. None of them ever has. None of them looks very hard.
rumor | Hollins Mill: 'a king buried twice' lies under the Ashward Barrows, west of the river farms.
@end

@scene N01_DROVER
say worker | I drive cattle up to Oathstone Crossroads. Tobiah Wend keeps a stall at the milestone.
say worker | Wend walks the whole March with that pack. Tell him Hollins sent you and he'll still overcharge you, but kindly.
@end

@scene N01_REFUGEE
say volunteer | We walked from Veyr when the square fell in. Hollin met us on the road with a cart of flour and didn't ask for a chit.
say volunteer | I'd never seen a mill. It's just a wheel and some stubborn people. Turns out that's enough.
@end

@scene N01_SURVIVOR
say survivor | The fault went through the toll road north of here. The Gallowgate fort's on an island now. Serves it right.
@end

@scene N01_INN
if phase:post post
say keeper | The Millstone. Bed, bread and a fire. Thirty crowns, and you can pay the Crown's share to me instead.
inn 30
end
label post
say keeper | Beds are free for anyone walking out of the ruin. You're walking out of it, aren't you? Close enough.
inn 0
@end

@scene N01_INN_DROVER
say worker | Cattle sleep standing up. Drovers sleep lying down, whenever they can. Go away.
@end

@scene N01_CARDS
say worker | We play for Crown chits. Nobody wants to win. The winner has to spend them at the toll canteen.
@end

@scene N01_SHOPKEEP
say keeper | Linden's Store. Tonics, rope, good boots, a sword or two from the old garrison. Look, then haggle.
shop SHOP_N01
@end

@scene N01_CRAFTER
if flag:n01_juniper met
set n01_juniper
say juniper | You've got the look of people who break things. Good. I mend them, and sometimes I make them better.
say juniper | Bring me materials - ore from the hills, hides, herbs, scrap from anything the Crown leaves lying about.
say juniper | A bench can't make something from nothing. But it can make a lot from a little. That's the whole trick of this village.
end
label met
say juniper | Iron ore, hides, pond reeds, Crown scrap. Anything with a bit of use left in it. I'll show you what it wants to be.
@end

@scene N01_CUSTOMER
say baker | Linden charges the Crown price and then gives you a biscuit. I can't decide if that's kindness or cheek.
@end

@scene N01_NELL
if phase:post post
say nell | Stones are dressed, the race is open, the wheel's greased. Grandad talks. I grind.
say nell | If the tithe clerk says we're short again, I'll grind him.
end
label post
say nell | We grind for three villages now. Refugees carry the sacks. Nobody takes a share. I didn't know flour could feel like this.
@end

@scene N01_CLERK
say clerk | Tithe clerk. Every seventh sack is the Crown's. Every tenth after that is also the Crown's. It's in the schedule.
@end

@scene N01_SACKMAN
say worker | Mind the millstones. They took my brother's thumb in the spring. He says it's the most honest tax he's paid.
@end

@scene N01_GRAN
say elder | Hollin's mother planted that garden across the race. Beans, sage, gloves. Don't ask about the gloves.
say elder | He won't cross the bridge since she passed. Somebody should. Things want to be found.
@end

# ================================================================ N02 Gallowgate Toll (R01 landmark, CH02)
@scene N02_TOLL
say guard | Halt. Gallowgate Toll. Ten crowns a head, a Crown writ, or turn round and walk home.
if party:C08 sak
choice | Show Raven's old commission -> writ | Turn back -> back
label sak
choice | Show Raven's old commission -> writ | Let Sak talk -> forge | Turn back -> back
label writ
say dain | Raven. Crown executioner, convoy service. The commission's still on file.
say guard | ...Sir. Yes, sir. Go through, sir.
say dain | The name still opens doors. I hate that it does.
set n02_toll
end
label forge
say pip | Ministry of Provision, audit division. You're three tallies short on the east ledger. Don't get up.
say guard | Audit. Right. Go on through. Please don't mention the ledger to the sergeant.
set n02_toll
end
label back
say guard | Sensible.
move player down
@end

@scene N02_TOLLMAN
if flag:n02_toll paid
say guard | Ten crowns, a writ, or the road home. I don't make the rules. I just stand in front of them.
end
label paid
say guard | You're through. The canteen takes chits and coin. The bounty board takes everybody's name eventually.
@end

@scene N02_CARTER
say worker | Three days in the queue with a cart of turnips. The turnips are winning.
say worker | They weigh the load, then weigh me, then charge me for the difference. I asked what the difference was. 'Attitude.'
@end

@scene N02_CARTER2
say worker | I haul slag bricks from Cinder Reach. They tax me at both ends of the toll road and once in the middle for luck.
say worker | Past Cinderwake there's a village called Kettle Row, right under a dam. Nice people. Terrible dam.
rumor | Gallowgate: Kettle Row, a slag-miners' village in Cinder Reach, sits right under a failing dam.
@end

@scene N02_HOODED
say survivor | Don't look at me. Look at the carts. I'm a cart.
say survivor | ...My name's on the board in there. I sold Crown grain back to the people who grew it. Cheaply. That's the crime.
@end

@scene N02_KENNELMAN
if flag:n02_hounds done
say worker | Toll hounds. The Crown breeds them to smell coin in a pocket. Four broke loose in the run and I'm not going in.
say worker | Sergeant Kell's posted it on the board. Somebody braver than me can collect.
end
label done
say worker | You cleared the run. I'll raise the next litter kinder. Maybe. They're still toll hounds.
@end

@scene N02_BARRACKS
say guard | Barracks. Crown personnel only. That includes me, barely. They lost my papers in Veyr.
@end

@scene N02_WEND
say wend | The queue's the best market in the March. Nobody can leave, and everybody's bored.
shop SHOP_WEND
@end

@scene N02_BOUNTY
if flag:n02_paid paid
if flag:n02_bounty taken
say narr | Notices are nailed three deep. WANTED FOR QUESTIONING: a list of names from Hollins Mill and Wren's Orchard.
say narr | Under them, a work notice: 'Toll hounds loose in the kennel run. 150 crowns on clearing it. See Sgt. Kell.'
choice | Take the hound notice -> take | Tear down the villagers' names -> tear | Leave it -> bye
label take
set n02_bounty
say narr | You pull the hound notice. The kennel run is behind the fence, east of the road.
if flag:n02_names_torn bye
choice | Tear down the names too -> tear | Leave them -> bye
label tear
if flag:n02_names_torn bye
set n02_names_torn
say narr | The 'persons of interest' come down in one handful. Somebody in the cart queue laughs once and then very carefully stops.
label bye
end
label taken
say narr | The hound notice is gone from its nail. The kennel run is east of the road, behind the fence.
end
label paid
say narr | New notices already. 'Bandit on the Cinder road.' 'Bells ringing in the marsh chapel: investigate, NOT urgent.'
rumor | Gallowgate bounty board: bells ringing at the marsh chapel east of Wren's Orchard. Marked 'not urgent'.
@end

@scene N02_KENNEL
say narr | Four toll hounds turn from a torn sack of coin-scented rags. Their collars are stamped with the Ministry seal.
battle OWX_R01_2
set n02_hounds
say narr | The run is quiet. Sergeant Kell will want to hear it from you.
@end

@scene N02_WAYSTONE
if flag:waystone_n02 done
set waystone_n02
discover L_N02
sfx FX029
say narr | The waystone warms. Far to the west you feel the Oathstone answer.
say narr | (Waystone attuned: Gallowgate Toll.)
end
label done
say narr | The waystone hums under a film of road dust.
@end

@scene N02_HERMIT
say elder | I hid in the gatehouse when the ground split. When I came out the garrison was gone and the road ended in the sea.
say elder | Sergeant Kell took the soldiers who'd listen and went east. Said she was done collecting names. Good for her.
rumor | Gallowgate: Sergeant Kell deserted after the fault and took her soldiers east, toward the Glass Coast shoals.
@end

@scene N02_SCAV
say survivor | Picking the fort clean. Crown iron, Crown rope, Crown nails. The Crown won't miss it. The Crown's in the sea.
@end

@scene N02_COOK
say baker | Canteen. Crown chits or coin. The stew is mostly turnip, because of the queue outside.
shop SHOP_N02
@end

@scene N02_KELL
if flag:n02_paid after
if flag:n02_hounds pay
say kell | Sergeant Kell. I run the bounty board, and the toll, and the kennel, and my own patience, which is thinnest.
if flag:n02_bounty took
say kell | If you want work, the board's outside. If you want mercy, the chapel's in the marsh.
end
label took
say kell | You took the hound notice. The run's east of the road. They bite low. Keep your boots on.
end
label pay
set n02_paid
say kell | Kennel-master says the run's clear. Here. The Crown pays late, but I don't.
gold 150
give I018 1
if flag:n02_names_torn torn
end
label torn
say kell | Somebody tore down my persons-of-interest. Now I'll have to copy them all out again. Slowly. Very, very slowly.
end
label after
say kell | Twenty years collecting names. Some days I'd like to hand one back.
@end

@scene N02_CLERK
say clerk | Bounty office. I pay out, I log it, I file it. The file goes to Veyr. Nobody in Veyr reads it. I checked.
@end

@scene N02_SOLDIER
say guard | Off shift. We play 'guess the toll'. The sergeant makes up a new number every morning. I'm on a streak.
@end

@scene N02_SOLDIER2
say guard | I joined to guard a road. Turns out a toll is a road with a fist in it.
@end

@scene N02_SQUATTER
say survivor | We sleep in the canteen now. The cook stayed. The stew's still turnip. Some things survive anything.
@end

# ================================================================ N03 Wren's Orchard (R01 village, CH03) + the ghost orchard (night event)
# There is no night condition for NPCs, so the ghost orchard is gated on flag n03_night, set by ringing the grove bell
# "and waiting for dark" (N03_BELL). The event closes with n03_ghosts_done (N03_WREN).
@scene N03_WREN
if flag:n03_ghosts_done after
if flag:n03_harl resolve
if phase:post post
say wren | Wren Tamsey. It's my orchard, whatever the levy officer writes on his slate.
say wren | We press in autumn and pay in spring. The Crown takes a cask in three and calls the rest 'surplus'.
say wren | The old grove behind the bell gate is shut. My Harl's buried there, with the pickers from the levy night. Leave them be.
end
label post
say wren | The fault shook every apple off every tree in one night. We pressed for a week. The whole March drank.
say wren | The levy officer went back to Veyr. There's no Veyr to go back to. I hope he found somewhere.
end
label resolve
say wren | You went into the old grove after dark. ...You saw him. Don't tell me you didn't, you've got the look.
say dain | He said the deed to the press is under the third press stone. He hid it the night of the levy, so the Crown couldn't take the farm.
say wren | Under the stone. Twelve years I've paid rent to the Crown on my own orchard because I couldn't find that paper.
say wren | ...That fool. That lovely, stubborn fool. He could have just told me before he went out picking in the frost.
set n03_ghosts_done
tint none
say wren | Take this. It was his militia pennant. He'd want it on somebody who goes out at night on purpose.
give A101 1
give I021 1
journal | Wren's Orchard: the ghosts of the levy night are at rest. Wren has the deed to her press.
end
label after
say wren | The deed's framed over the press. The levy officer read it twice and went very quiet. Best cider we've made in years.
@end

@scene N03_MOURNER
if flag:n03_ghosts_done done
say elder | Twelve autumns back, the Crown wanted the harvest in before the frost. The levy made the pickers work through the night.
say elder | Six of them froze in the old grove. Harl Wren was one. Now the bell at the gate rings itself on cold nights.
say elder | The pickers say if you ring it at dusk and wait, you'll see them working. I've never had the nerve.
rumor | Wren's Orchard: ring the old grove bell at dusk and wait, and the levy-night pickers come back.
end
label done
say elder | The bell hasn't rung by itself since you went in there. I miss it, a little. Isn't that odd.
@end

@scene N03_BELL
if flag:n03_ghosts_done rest
if flag:n03_night dark
say narr | The rope is tied off with a black ribbon. The pickers say: ring it at dusk, then wait.
choice | Ring the bell and wait for dark -> ring | Leave it -> bye
label ring
sfx FX009
fade out 0.8
set n03_night
wait 0.6
fade in 0.8
tint #0b1530a0
say narr | Night comes down the rows. Frost ticks on the grass. Past the gate, lanterns move between the dead trees.
say narr | Nobody is holding them.
end
label dark
say narr | The bell is still swinging a little. It is fully dark in the old grove.
end
label rest
say narr | The ribbon is gone. Somebody has tied a sprig of apple blossom to the rope instead.
label bye
@end

@scene N03_WISPS once
say narr | The lanterns turn toward you all at once. They are not lanterns.
battle OWX_R01_3
say narr | The wisps scatter into the frost. Deeper in, a man in a picker's smock is still working, one hand on an empty basket.
@end

@scene N03_HARL
if flag:n03_harl told
say harl | Mind the frost. You'll lose your fingers. We did.
say harl | The levy said the harvest had to be in before dawn. Crown's orders. So we picked by lamplight, and the cold came.
say dain | You're Harl Wren.
say harl | I was. Is Wren still pressing? Tell her - no. Tell her something useful.
say harl | The deed to the press is under the third press stone. I hid it that night so the levy couldn't take the farm if we didn't finish.
say harl | We didn't finish. She's been paying the Crown rent ever since, hasn't she? Tell her. Then we can put the baskets down.
set n03_harl
objective harl | Tell Wren Tamsey that the press deed is under the third press stone.
end
label told
say harl | Tell Wren. The third stone. Then we can put the baskets down.
@end

@scene N03_GHOST1
say survivor | Two more rows. Two more rows and they said we could go in. That was a long time ago, wasn't it.
@end

@scene N03_GHOST2
say survivor | The levy man had a warm coat. I remember his coat better than my own mother's face.
@end

@scene N03_GHOST3
say child | I was only here to carry the baskets. Harl gave me his gloves. He shouldn't have. His hands went first.
@end

@scene N03_GRAVE
say narr | Six names are cut into a flat stone. The last two are cut shallower, as if the mason ran out of daylight, or money.
if flag:n03_ghosts_done rest
end
label rest
say narr | Someone has left six cups of cider on the stone.
@end

@scene N03_PRESSSTONE
if flag:n03_ghosts_done done
if flag:n03_harl found
say narr | The third stone sits a finger higher than the others. It won't budge. It's probably nothing.
end
label found
say narr | Under the third stone, where Harl said: an oilcloth bundle, dry as the day it was hidden. Wren should open it.
end
label done
say narr | The press stones are level now. The deed hangs framed on the wall above them.
@end

@scene N03_PICKER
say worker | Pick with a twist, not a pull. Pull, and you take next year's bud with it. The Crown pulls.
@end

@scene N03_PICKER2
if phase:post post
say worker | We send the best casks to Veyr, the worst to the toll, and drink the middling ones ourselves. Middling's lovely.
end
label post
say worker | The trees on the east rows split in the fault. The west rows are fine. We're grafting the one onto the other.
@end

@scene N03_CHILD
say child | My big brother says the dead people in the old grove come out if you ring the bell. I'd never. Would you?
@end

@scene N03_LEVY
if flag:n03_ghosts_done deed
say clerk | Harvest levy office. One cask in three. The widow Tamsey owes rent on the press. There's no deed on file.
say clerk | No deed, no farm. The Crown is patient. The Crown is also the only one with a pen.
end
label deed
say clerk | She has a deed. A real one. Dated before the levy. I... will need to consult the regional office. For some years.
@end

@scene N03_CARTER
say worker | Cider to Veyr, empties back. The toll at Gallowgate charges me for the empties. They're full of air. Crown air.
@end

@scene N03_BEEKEEPER
say elder | Bees don't pay tithe. They sting the assessor, and he writes them down as 'hazard', and then they're exempt.
say elder | Southeast, where the marsh starts, there's a chapel under the water. My bees won't fly over it.
rumor | Wren's Orchard: the beekeeper's bees won't fly over the drowned chapel in the marsh south-east.
@end

@scene N03_TASTER
say worker | I'm the taster. It's a real job. I taste, I nod, Wren tells me to stop nodding and start carrying.
@end

@scene N03_GUARD
say guard | Levy escort. I carry the officer's slate and his umbrella. Mostly the umbrella. It's a hard posting.
@end

@scene N03_REFUGEE
say survivor | I walked from Veyr with my shoes in my hand. Wren gave me a bed in the press loft and a job pulling apples.
say survivor | Twist, not pull. I'm learning.
@end

@scene N03_ORPHAN
say child | Posy says I can stay till my mum comes. Mum was at the square in Veyr. Posy says the orchard is good at waiting.
@end

@scene N03_INN
if phase:post post
say keeper | The Bent Bough. Bed, bread and a jug. Thirty-five crowns, cider included, headache extra.
inn 35
end
label post
say keeper | Pay what you can. Most pay in apples. We have a great many apples.
inn 5
@end

@scene N03_GUEST
say worker | Cinderwake smith, taking the long road home. The toll road's too dear. The orchard road's too pleasant. I'm torn.
@end

@scene N03_GUEST2
say buyer | I'm a buyer from Veyr. I taste, I bargain, I lose. Wren always wins. It's why I keep coming back.
@end

@scene N03_SHOPKEEP
say duro | Cellarman Duro. Casks, tonics, tools for the rows, and a few blades the pickers use for pruning. And for wolves.
shop SHOP_N03
@end

@scene N03_CRAFTER
if flag:n03_mab met
set n03_mab
say mab | I'm the cooper. I make casks and I mend everything else. Bring me hardwood, hoops, hides. Iron if you can find it.
say mab | A good barrel is a hundred small promises, all holding. Armour's the same idea, only it's rude about it.
end
label met
say mab | Hardwood, iron hoops, hides, orchard resin. Bring them and I'll bend something useful out of them.
@end

@scene N03_PRESSBOY
say apprentice | Don't touch the third press stone. It rocks. Duro says it's always rocked. I think something's under it.
@end

@scene N03_DAUGHTER
if flag:n03_ghosts_done after
say child | Mum says Dad's in the old grove. I don't go in there. The bell does it for me.
end
label after
say child | Mum laughed today, then she cried, then she laughed again. She says it was about a piece of paper.
@end

@scene N03_AUNT
say elder | I came to help my sister after Harl died. That was twelve years ago. I'm still helping. Help takes a long time.
@end

# ================================================================ N04 The Sunken Chapel (R01 optional dungeon, CH03; boss BX01 Ser Hallam)
# Mechanism: the chapel bells. The west bell rope (nave) lifts the cloister portcullis; the east bell (cloister island)
# raises the reliquary grille. A barred side door in the cloister is a shortcut back to the porch.
@scene N04_ENTER once
say narr | The marsh gives way to flagstones. The chapel stands up to its knees in black water, doors open.
say narr | Somewhere inside, a bell rings once. The water around your boots trembles with it.
say dain | Oath-knights built these. They swore to hold them. I knew the words once.
@end

@scene N04_BELL_W
if flag:n04_bell_w done
set n04_bell_w
sfx FX009
shake 0.3
say narr | You haul on the west bell rope. Far overhead, a drowned bell tolls. The west portcullis grinds up out of the silt.
end
label done
say narr | The rope is slack. The west portcullis stays up.
@end

@scene N04_BELL_E
if flag:n04_bell_e done
set n04_bell_e
sfx FX009
shake 0.4
say narr | The island bell is only a hand bell on a chain. You ring it anyway. Every bell in the chapel answers, underwater.
say narr | Back in the nave, iron shrieks. Something has lifted.
end
label done
say narr | The little bell hangs quiet on its chain.
@end

@scene N04_BAR
if flag:n04_shortcut done
set n04_shortcut
sfx FX010
say narr | You lift the bar off the side door. It opens onto the marsh porch, where you came in.
end
label done
say narr | The bar leans against the wall. The side door is open.
@end

@scene N04_PILGRIM
say survivor | I came to pray for my son. The water came in during the hymn. I'm still waiting for the hymn to end.
say survivor | The knights still stand their watch below. Their commander will not let anyone pass who cannot relieve him.
@end

@scene N04_AUBEL
if flag:n04_cleared after
say aubel | Squire Aubel, Ser Hallam's shield-bearer. I drowned carrying his shield. He's been carrying it since.
say aubel | He swore to hold the chapel until relieved. Nobody came. Now he holds it against everyone, even the water.
say aubel | When he raises the shield, don't strike. Wait, or break the oath with storm. Lightning goes through wet steel.
say aubel | And when the bells ring underwater, brace. He floods the altar and the cold slows you to a crawl.
end
label after
say aubel | You relieved him. That's all any of us wanted. Somebody to say: you can stop now.
say aubel | Tell whoever's giving the orders these days. Somebody should say it to them, too.
@end

@scene N04_RELIC
say narr | Forty oath-rings in a velvet tray. One slot is empty. Whoever wore it is still upstairs, holding.
@end

@scene N04_BOSS once
say narr | The knight at the altar turns. Water pours from the joints of his armour. His visor is full of it.
say hallam | Halt. This chapel is held by the Order of the Oath. None pass until the garrison is relieved.
say dain | Ser. The Order's gone. The Crown dissolved it. There's no one left to relieve you.
say hallam | Then I hold. I hold until relieved. I HOLD.
say narr | Every bell in the chapel rings at once, underwater.
battle BX01 noflee
set n04_cleared
say narr | Hallam goes to one knee. The water runs out of his armour and does not come back.
say hallam | ...You are relieved, Ser Hallam. Is that it? Did someone say it?
say dain | I'm saying it. You're relieved. Stand down.
say hallam | Thank you. Take the plate. It never kept the water out. Perhaps it will keep something else.
say narr | The bells stop. For the first time in years, the chapel is quiet.
journal | The Sunken Chapel: Ser Hallam is relieved of his watch. The drowned bells have stopped.
@end

@scene N04_ALTAR
if flag:n04_cleared quiet
say narr | The altar is under a finger of water. The stone is carved with the Order's oath: WE HOLD UNTIL RELIEVED.
end
label quiet
say narr | Someone has cut a new line under the oath, fresh and shallow: RELIEVED.
@end

# ================================================================ N05 Ashward Barrows (R01 secret, CH06) + QC14 first beat
# The barrow door answers to the first king's oath, cut on the fifth face of the Oathstone (N41_MILESTONE sets n41_oath).
@scene N05_WARDEN
if flag:n05_open opened
say edrin | Barrow-warden. I keep the grass short and the curious out. The first is easier.
say edrin | The big barrow has a speaking stone. It wants the oath of the first king. Nobody alive knows it.
say edrin | Well. The Crown's masons keep chiselling it off the Oathstone at the crossroads. So somebody knows it.
rumor | Ashward Barrows: the barrow door opens to the first king's oath, cut on the Oathstone at the crossroads.
end
label opened
say edrin | You opened it. I've kept that grass for forty years and never once seen inside. ...Don't tell me. I like the not knowing.
@end

@scene N05_PILGRIM
say survivor | The fault shook half the March into the sea and didn't crack one stone out here. The barrows are stubborn.
say survivor | People come to lay flowers at the round door now. They don't know whose it is. It doesn't seem to matter.
@end

@scene N05_STONE
say narr | A crown carved on the stone, chiselled out, then carved again, badly, by a different hand.
say narr | Under it, very small: 'CROWNED TWICE. BURIED TWICE.'
@end

@scene N05_DOOR
say narr | A round stone blocks the barrow. Around its rim, worn half away: '...SHALL WEIGH MORE THAN THE HEAD...'
say narr | The stone seems to be waiting for the rest.
if flag:n41_oath know
choice | Speak: 'Long live the Crown.' -> wrong | Speak: 'The crown is the realm.' -> wrong | Leave it -> bye
label know
choice | Speak: 'No crown shall weigh more than the head that bears it.' -> right | Speak: 'Long live the Crown.' -> wrong | Leave it -> bye
label right
set n05_open
sfx FX009
shake 0.4
say narr | The words go into the stone like a key. It rolls aside with a sound like someone sighing in their sleep.
end
label wrong
say narr | The stone does not move. Somewhere under the grass, something laughs, once, without any breath.
label bye
@end

@scene N05_WIGHTS once
say narr | The dust in the passage stands up. Two knights in ash-grey mail, their faces long gone, level their swords.
say narr | They wear no Crown seal. Their tabards bear a crown with a line struck through it.
battle OWX_R01_4
say narr | The wights fold back into dust. The passage to the inner barrow is open.
@end

@scene N05_TOMB
if recruited:C14 maldrath
say narr | The sarcophagus is empty. In its lid, a crown-shaped hollow, polished smooth, as if something was lifted out and put back many times.
say narr | Scratched inside the lid, where only the dead could read it: 'THEY CARRIED THE CROWN BACK DOWN TO HIM WHO WORE IT FIRST. I WILL FOLLOW IT.'
end
label maldrath
if flag:qc14_barrows known
set qc14_barrows
say maldrath | My knights buried me here the first time. Without the crown. The priests carried it back down to the one who wore it first.
say maldrath | The First Crown. The first head it ever sat on. They entombed him with it, deep under the quarry. Every king after only borrowed it.
say maldrath | I borrowed it. I sent people into that quarry with it on my head. Then they took it back, and put me here with the grass.
say dain | Then we find where it went.
say maldrath | Below. Past the dragons and the builders, in the country of the dead. His tomb. I can feel it from here, like a bad tooth.
quest QC14 ACTIVE barrows
journal | QC14 The First Crown: Maldrath's first grave lies empty in the Ashward Barrows. The crown went below - to the First Crown's Tomb, deep in the Hollow Throne.
objective maldrath | Find the First Crown's Tomb in the realm of the dead, below the Deep.
end
label known
say maldrath | Empty, still. The tomb that matters is below. The Hollow Throne. I can feel it pulling.
@end

# ================================================================ N06 Kettle Row (R02 village, CH04; destroyed in the fault)
@scene N06_GRETE
if flag:n42_cleared dam
say grete | Grete Ossery, forewoman of the Slag Guild. Two hundred souls under that dam, and the Crown says it's sound.
say grete | The Crown inspected it from the far bank, with a telescope, in fog. We have the certificate framed in the hall.
say grete | If you're going up to the Weeping Dam, go quiet. There's something living in the sluice works. The keeper's widow went in and never came out.
rumor | Kettle Row: something lives in the Weeping Dam's sluice works, north of the village.
end
label dam
say grete | The seep's slowed since you went up the dam. It still weeps. But it weeps like a person now, not like a flood.
say grete | I've told the guild to cut a second road out of the valley. Just in case. Nobody laughed.
@end

@scene N06_PICKETT
if flag:n06_taught known
set n06_taught
say pickett | New hands? Good. I teach every new miner the same way: here's a seam, here's a pick, don't die.
say pickett | See the chalk marks on the ledge? That's a node. Face it and work it and it'll give up ore. Not forever. It grows back slow.
say pickett | Iron for a smith's first upgrade. Heartsteel deeper down, in hot places. Starmetal only where the sky's fallen in.
say pickett | Take the pick. We've more picks than miners these days. That's not a joke. I wish it was.
give M001 1
end
label known
say pickett | Nodes along the ledge, marked in chalk. Work them, let them rest, come back. The mountain's patient. Be patient back.
@end

@scene N06_NODE1
if flag:n06_node1 done
set n06_node1
sfx FX010
say narr | You work the chalk-marked seam. A fist of dull ore breaks free.
give M001 1
end
label done
say narr | The seam is worked out for now. It will grow back, slowly.
@end

@scene N06_NODE2
if flag:n06_node2 done
set n06_node2
sfx FX010
say narr | The glinting seam gives up two good lumps of ore and a shower of grit.
give M001 2
end
label done
say narr | Someone has chalked a new mark: 'RESTING'.
@end

@scene N06_CHILD
say child | I'm king of the slag heap! My dad says slag's what's left when you take the good out of rock. I'm still king of it.
@end

@scene N06_DAMWATCH
say sallow | I watch the dam. Somebody ought to. I count the weeps. Forty years ago it was three. Today it was thirty-one.
say sallow | The keeper's widow went up there when her man drowned in the sluices. They say she's in the walls now, holding it together out of spite.
@end

@scene N06_FOREMAN
say clerk | Crown foreman. Output is down nine percent. The guild says it's the coughing. I have noted 'coughing' as an excuse.
say clerk | The dam is certified. Stop looking at it like that.
@end

@scene N06_CARTMAN
say worker | Carts to Cinderwake, slag bricks back. Bricks for the Crown's new furnaces. Furnaces for more slag. It's a wheel.
@end

@scene N06_WIDOW
say baker | Washing doesn't dry here. It just gets differently grey. I hang it out anyway. You have to keep some rules.
@end

@scene N06_SMOKER
say worker | Pipe? No? Everyone in Kettle Row smokes. Makes the air taste like something you chose.
@end

@scene N06_PROSPECTOR
say elder | West of here the old slag runs down the terraces like a river, still hot. The Slagfalls. Something walks up there.
say elder | North, in the ridges, there's Anvil Cairn - the smiths' shrine. The old forge-mother there will temper a blade for a song. A good song.
rumor | Kettle Row: the Slagfalls to the west still run hot, and something big walks the terraces.
rumor | Kettle Row: Anvil Cairn, the smiths' shrine in the northern ridges, keeps an old forge-mother who tempers blades.
@end

@scene N06_KID2
say child | Grandad says if the dam ever breaks, run uphill and don't look back. I practise every day. I'm very fast now.
@end

@scene N06_GUARD
say guard | I guard the road out. Nobody leaves Kettle Row owing the Crown. So, nobody leaves Kettle Row.
@end

@scene N06_COUGH
say patient | Slag lung. Everybody gets it. The Crown doctor says fresh air. I asked him where. He's still thinking.
@end

@scene N06_INN
say keeper | The Slag and Kettle. Soup, a bed, a window that doesn't face the dam. The window's extra. Forty crowns.
inn 40
@end

@scene N06_PATRON
say worker | We toast the dam every night. 'To the dam: hold one more day.' It's held forty years of days. Cheers.
@end

@scene N06_PATRON2
say worker | Pipewright's Rest, up the viaduct, does a better stew. Don't tell Bessa. She'll put slag in mine.
rumor | Kettle Row: Pipewright's Rest, the pipe-inn on the viaduct north-east, does the best stew in the Reach.
@end

@scene N06_SHOPKEEP
say keeper | Guild smithy. Picks, hammers, and anything else you can swing. Ore, if you've got the coin. Tonics, because we must.
shop SHOP_N06
@end

@scene N06_CRAFTER
if flag:n06_ivy met
set n06_ivy
say ivy | I'm Ivy. The guild calls me Ember-hand, because I don't use tongs. I don't recommend it.
say ivy | Bring me ore and I'll make it into something that keeps you alive. Iron first. Heartsteel from the hot places, if you're brave.
say ivy | Slag's no good. Slag is what the Crown leaves you. Bring me what it doesn't know it's missing.
end
label met
say ivy | Iron ore, heartsteel, cinder hides, pipe brass. Bring it and I'll put it to work.
@end

@scene N06_APPRENTICE
say apprentice | I quench the blades in the trough. The water comes down from the dam. Harrow says that makes them brave.
@end

@scene N06_CHAIR
say worker | Guild Chair Dunn. We meet every week to vote on whether to ask the Crown to fix the dam. We always vote yes.
say worker | We've sent forty-one letters. They send one back every year: 'Received.' At least they're polite.
@end

@scene N06_MINER1
say worker | I move that we stop writing letters and start digging a second road. Seconded? No? Next week, then.
@end

@scene N06_MINER2
say worker | I've got four children and a rock in my chest. The rock's the only one that pays rent.
@end

@scene N06_MINER3
say patient | Don't mind the cough. It's how you know a Kettle Row man's still breathing.
@end

@scene N06_HALLCLERK
say clerk | Minutes of the Slag Guild. Item one: the dam. Item two: the dam. Item three: tea.
@end

# ================================================================ N07 The Slagfalls (R02 optional dungeon, CH04; boss BX02 Slag Colossus)
# Mechanism: cooling vents. Vent One chills the slag ford on the lower terrace; Vent Two (on the crucible island) clears
# the upper stair. A clinker-choked slag chute is the shortcut back to the foot.
@scene N07_ENTER once
say narr | The mountainside is a staircase of black glass. Molten slag slides down it in slow orange sheets.
say narr | High above, something the size of a house takes a step, and the whole terrace shivers.
@end

@scene N07_GUIDE
if flag:n07_cleared after
say worker | Slag runner. I carry messages across the falls for the guild. You learn where the crust holds.
say worker | The vents cool the fords if you open them. The guild built them when the Crown's flue started pouring. Nobody maintains them.
say worker | Up top, the overflow's got legs. We call it the Colossus. It's just slag that nobody told to stop.
end
label after
say worker | The terraces are still hot, but nothing walks them. I can run the falls in half the time. Thank you.
@end

@scene N07_HERMIT
say survivor | The fault cracked the Crown's flue. The slag stopped. The falls are cooling. In ten years this'll be a hill.
say survivor | I live here now. It's warm, and nobody collects anything.
@end

@scene N07_VENT1
if flag:n07_vent1 done
set n07_vent1
sfx FX010
shake 0.3
say narr | The valve screams open. A blast of cold air roars down the channel, and the slag across the ford blackens and sets.
end
label done
say narr | The vent roars steadily. The ford stays black and solid.
@end

@scene N07_VENT2
if flag:n07_vent2 done
set n07_vent2
sfx FX010
shake 0.4
say narr | The crucible vent opens with a sound like a bellows the size of a church. Down on the terrace, the clinker over the upper stair cracks and falls away.
end
label done
say narr | Cold air pours down toward the terrace.
@end

@scene N07_CHUTE
if flag:n07_chute done
set n07_chute
sfx FX010
shake 0.3
say narr | You kick the chute gate open. A plug of cooled clinker tumbles away downhill. The chute runs straight to the foot of the falls.
end
label done
say narr | The chute is clear. It's a quick, hot slide to the foot.
@end

@scene N07_BONES
say survivor | Prospector. Came for heartsteel in the crucible. The slag came for me. It's all right. It's warm.
say survivor | The big one up top gets harder as it cools. Ice cracks it, but fire only feeds it. When its heart goes white, cover your faces.
@end

@scene N07_RUNNER
say worker | Twisted my ankle on the last climb. I'm waiting for the ankle to agree to go down. It hasn't yet.
say worker | If you're going up: the Colossus burns everyone in front of it. Keep cool cloths ready. And don't stand in the pour.
@end

@scene N07_FLUE
say narr | A Crown furnace flue juts from the ridge above, pouring slag down the mountain. It has been pouring for twenty years.
say narr | A brass plate on the flue reads: 'OVERFLOW - ROUTE TO NATURAL TERRAIN.'
@end

@scene N07_BOSS once
say narr | The slag at the top of the falls gathers itself. A body of cooling crust stands up, glowing at every crack.
say narr | It has no face. It has a Crown furnace seal pressed into its chest, still legible, still hot.
say dain | It's not alive. It's just the overflow, walking.
say ilyr | Walking toward the villages below. Stop it here.
battle BX02 noflee
set n07_cleared
say narr | The Colossus slumps, and cools, and cracks into a hill of black glass.
say narr | The flue above still pours, but the slag now runs into the crucible and stays there, like it has nowhere left to go.
journal | The Slagfalls: the Slag Colossus is broken. The overflow no longer walks toward Kettle Row.
@end

# ================================================================ N08 Anvil Cairn (R02 landmark, CH04; forge crafter crafter_n08)
@scene N08_SEO
if flag:n08_seo met
set n08_seo
say seo | Seo. They call me the Anvil-Mother, because I've outlived every smith who ever called me anything else.
say seo | You carry Crown steel. It's poor. They fold it twice and call it done. Bring me ore and I'll fold it properly.
if party:C04 golem
say seo | Take this for the road. Heartsteel, from the cairn. Pilgrims leave it. I don't need it; I've enough to finish me.
give M002 1
shop SHOP_N08
end
label golem
say seo | ...And you. A rune-engine. I knew the smiths who cast your plates. You're better kept than they were.
say ivo | Pell keeps me. I keep the rest.
say seo | Then take this, for Pell, and for the rest. Heartsteel. You'll know what to do with it.
give M002 1
shop SHOP_N08
end
label met
say seo | Iron for the first fold. Heartsteel for the second. Starmetal only if the sky gives it to you. And patience, always.
shop SHOP_N08
@end

@scene N08_STRIKER
say apprentice | I'm her striker. She points, I hit. Seven years. Last week she said 'better'. I'll remember it on my deathbed.
@end

@scene N08_QUENCH
say worker | Quench water comes from the cairn spring. The Crown's smiths use slag water. Their blades sing flat. Listen next time.
@end

@scene N08_KEEPER
if phase:post post
say cairnkeeper | Every smith of the Reach brings an anvil here once in their life. The cairn is a thousand anvils deep.
say cairnkeeper | The Crown sent assessors to count them for scrap. They counted to four hundred, then asked for a blessing and went home.
end
label post
say cairnkeeper | The fault shook the cairn. Not one anvil fell. Smiths from the drowned towns come to strike them, just to hear them ring.
@end

@scene N08_PILGRIM
say worker | Journeyman. I carried my father's anvil up the ridge on my back. Three days. He'd have said I was holding it wrong.
say worker | They say the dam over Kettle Row is failing. If it goes, half the smiths of the Reach go with it.
@end

@scene N08_WEND
say wend | Smiths are the only people in the March who haggle harder than me. I come here to be humbled.
shop SHOP_WEND
@end

@scene N08_REFUGEE
say survivor | Kettle Row's gone. We came up to the cairn because it was the highest place we knew. Seo feeds us. Seo feeds everyone.
@end

@scene N08_CHILD
say child | I'm going to be a smith. I've already got a hammer. It's a spoon, but it's a hammer inside.
@end

@scene N08_ANVIL
say narr | The Great Anvil stands in a ring of smaller ones. Its face is scarred with hundreds of smiths' marks.
if party:C04 golem
end
label golem
say narr | One mark, near the horn, is a rune. The Golem looks at it for a long time.
say ivo | That's my maker's mark. The same one that's on my heart-plate. I didn't know he came here.
say ivo | ...He struck the anvil twice. Pilgrims only strike once. The second strike means 'for someone else'.
@end

# ================================================================ N09 Pipewright's Rest (R02 village, CH05; lost in the fault)
@scene N09_INN
say dunmore | Dunmore Pipewright. Fifth of the name. My great-great-grandmother built the viaduct, then built an inn on it so the builders had somewhere to drink.
say dunmore | Stew, bed, hot bath. Forty-five crowns. The view's free, and so's the swaying.
inn 45
@end

@scene N09_BATHER
say worker | The bath's fed off the boiler pipes. It's the only hot water in the Reach the Crown doesn't meter. Don't tell them.
@end

@scene N09_PATRON
say worker | I come up from Cinderwake for the stew. And to feel the viaduct move under my chair. It reminds me I'm alive.
@end

@scene N09_SHOPKEEP
say keeper | Pim, agent for Tobiah Wend. He sends the goods, I sell them, he complains about my prices. It works.
shop SHOP_N09
@end

@scene N09_CRAFTER
if flag:n09_olen met
set n09_olen
say olen | Olen. Brasswife. I bend pipe into whatever you need. Gauntlets, greaves, a kettle that whistles your name.
say olen | Bring me brass from the Crown's scrap heaps and ore from the ridges. The viaduct taught me: good joints hold anything.
end
label met
say olen | Pipe brass, iron ore, cinder hides, boiler rivets. Bring them. I'll make them hold.
@end

@scene N09_MASTER
say hesta | Hesta. Master of the viaduct. Eleven hundred joints, and I know every one by its voice.
say hesta | The east piers are shifting. The Crown says it's the wind. It's the ground. Something under the Reach is moving.
rumor | Pipewright's Rest: the master pipewright says the ground under the Reach is moving, not the wind.
@end

@scene N09_APPRENTICE
say apprentice | Hesta makes me listen to the pipes for an hour every morning. I'm starting to hear them in my sleep. They're worried.
@end

@scene N09_FITTER
say worker | I fit the steam lines from the boilers to the town. If you hear a whistle, that's a leak. If you hear a scream, that's me.
@end

@scene N09_VALVE
say worker | Valve-watcher. I watch valves. It's a good job. Most of the time nothing happens, and I'm the one who knows it.
@end

@scene N09_INSPECTOR
say clerk | Crown pipe inspection. Every joint on the viaduct is taxed by the inch. The Pipewrights built very long joints.
@end

@scene N09_KID
say child | I drop stones off the edge and count. I've never heard one land. Old Ambrose says there's a river down there. I think it's the sky.
@end

@scene N09_LAUNDRESS
say baker | Laundry dries in an hour up here. The steam, the wind. It comes back grey, but dry. Grey and dry is a good day.
@end

@scene N09_AMBROSE
say ambrose | I laid the last stone on the east pier when I was nineteen. I'm ninety now. The pier's older than me and less tired.
say ambrose | Below, there's the river, and the old slag works, and the Weeping Dam. If that dam goes, this viaduct is all that stays dry.
@end

@scene N09_COURIER
say worker | Waiting for the steam lift down to the Cinderwake road. It comes when it comes. The lift has opinions.
@end

@scene N09_GUARD
say guard | Viaduct toll's been waived since the Crown couldn't find anyone willing to stand in the wind to collect it. Except me. For some reason.
@end

@scene N09_PIPER
say apprentice | I play the steam organ at noon. One chord. People say it's beautiful. It's the only chord it has.
@end

@scene N09_COOK
say baker | Stall stew, half the inn's price and twice the pepper. Dunmore says I'm stealing his trade. I say I'm stealing his pepper.
@end

@scene N09_VETERAN
say survivor | Crown pension. Eight crowns a month for a leg. The other leg's still paying the Crown back, apparently.
@end

# ================================================================ N10 Cindermaw Caldera (R02 secret, CH10; later the lair of SB01 Cindermaw)
# The lair stays sealed (block if=!flag:cindermaw_awake). The superboss itself is placed by the lead.
@scene N10_SEAL
say narr | A slab of cooled glass plugs the cave mouth, smooth as a pane and warm as skin.
say narr | Behind it, very slowly, something breathes. Each breath fogs the glass from the inside. Each breath takes a minute.
say ilyr | Do not knock.
say dain | You know what that is.
say ilyr | The oldest of us who never gave up its fragment. It sleeps. It wakes to eat towns. Let it sleep until you are strong enough to be rude.
rumor | Cindermaw Caldera: something ancient sleeps behind the glass seal in the crater. Ilyr says not to knock.
@end

@scene N10_SCHOLAR
say imre | Imre, fire-reader. I read the caldera's moods from the colour of the lake. Today it's 'irritable'.
say imre | The lake rises and falls once a minute, like breathing. The Crown sends surveyors. The surveyors don't come back to argue.
say imre | Whatever is behind the northern seal has slept for a thousand years. I've written a paper. Nobody reads papers about things that eat towns.
@end

@scene N10_WATCHER
say survivor | The fault split the caldera rim, but the seal held. I camp here to watch it. Someone should know first, if it opens.
say survivor | Some nights the glass glows. I pretend it's a lighthouse.
@end

# ================================================================ N41 Oathstone Crossroads (R01 landmark, CH02)
@scene N41_WEND
if phase:post post
if flag:wend_met again
set wend_met
say wend | Tobiah Wend, pack-merchant of the whole March. I sell rope, tonics, and news, and only the rope is guaranteed.
say wend | I walk a circuit: here, the Gallowgate queue, the smiths at Anvil Cairn. Look for the man with the ugliest pack.
shop SHOP_WEND
end
label again
say wend | Back again. The Oathstone brings everyone back eventually. That's what it's for.
shop SHOP_WEND
end
label post
say wend | Still walking. The roads broke, so I walk the pieces. Somebody has to carry the news between the islands.
say wend | Refuge Rock, out on the Glass Coast shoals - they'll need everything I've got. You'll find me there too, some days.
shop SHOP_WEND
@end

@scene N41_COURIER
say worker | Crown courier. Veyr to Gallowgate to Cinderwake and back, twice a week. I carry orders I'm not allowed to read.
say worker | I read them anyway. This week: more tithe, fewer excuses, and 'locate the Hollins gloves'. I don't know either.
@end

@scene N41_WARDEN
say guard | Road warden. The Crown built the Oathstone as a milestone. The towns built the market around it before the mortar dried.
say guard | Every road here has a toll further on. Gallowgate east is the worst. Carry coin or carry a good story.
@end

@scene N41_PILGRIM
if phase:post post
say elder | I walk to the Oathstone every spring and read the fifth face. It's an oath the first king swore to the March.
say elder | 'No crown shall weigh more than the head that bears it.' The Crown's masons keep trying to chisel it off. It keeps being legible.
end
label post
say elder | The fifth face cracked in the fault. The oath's still there. Just in two pieces, like everything.
@end

@scene N41_MASON
if phase:post post
say worker | I'm paid to recut the milestone every year. New distances, new Ministry seal. Old words underneath.
say worker | Found a carved box in the yard once, left for travellers. I left it there. Take what you need; leave the box.
end
label post
say worker | Nobody pays me now. I recut it anyway. The distances all changed. The roads go into the sea.
@end

@scene N41_CHILD
if phase:post post
say child | Water, one copper! It's well water, not Crown water. Crown water costs two coppers and tastes like the lid.
end
label post
say child | The well's still good! Mum says it's the only thing in the March the fault didn't touch. I'm not charging anymore.
@end

@scene N41_WAYKEEP
say keeper | Waystones hum when someone who's walked far touches them. Lay a hand on this one and it'll remember you.
say keeper | There are others. Gallowgate has one in the cart queue. The old keepers used them before the Crown numbered the roads.
@end

@scene N41_CAMP
say survivor | We came up from the rift edge. Veyr's half under water. The Oathstone's the tallest thing left standing for a day's walk.
say survivor | People leave notes on it. 'Gone to Hollins.' 'Gone to the Rock.' 'Wait for me.'
@end

@scene N41_CAMP2
say volunteer | I'm copying the notes off the milestone into a book, before the rain gets them. Names first. Then where they went.
@end

@scene N41_MILESTONE
say narr | North: VEYR, CROWN QUARRY. East: GALLOWGATE TOLL, WREN'S ORCHARD. West: THE ASH BARROWS. South: HOLLINS MILL.
say narr | The fifth face, low down and half-chiselled: NO CROWN SHALL WEIGH MORE THAN THE HEAD THAT BEARS IT.
set n41_oath
if phase:post notes
end
label notes
say narr | Paper notes are nailed all over the stone. 'Gone to Refuge Rock.' 'Gone to Hollins.' 'Mira - wait here.'
@end

@scene N41_WAYSTONE
if flag:waystone_n41 done
set waystone_n41
discover L_N41
sfx FX029
say narr | The waystone warms under your hand. For a moment you can feel the other stones along the roads, like far lamps.
say narr | (Waystone attuned: Oathstone Crossroads.)
end
label done
say narr | The waystone hums. It remembers you.
@end

# ================================================================ N42 The Weeping Dam (R02 optional dungeon, CH05; boss BX11 The Sluicewife)
# Mechanism: sluice levers. Sluice One drains the gallery walkway; Sluice Two (out on the valve race) opens the keeper's
# stair. A bolted maintenance hatch in the valve hall is the shortcut to the dam foot. Post-fault the dam has burst.
@scene N42_ENTER once
say narr | The dam fills half the sky. Water weeps from a hundred seams in its face and runs down to the roofs of Kettle Row.
say narr | Somewhere inside it, very faintly, a woman is humming.
@end

@scene N42_SAPPER
if flag:n42_cleared after
say worker | Guild sapper. They sent me to 'assess'. I've assessed. It's going to break. I'd like to go home now.
say worker | The service door's open. Something inside keeps the water back and the valves turning. It doesn't like visitors.
end
label after
say worker | The weeping's slowed. It's not fixed. Nothing is fixed. But it's slowed, and the guild's cutting a second road.
@end

@scene N42_MOURNER
say elder | Kettle Row was down there. The dam went when the ground broke. Two hundred people and the valley's a lake now.
say elder | Some got up the high road Grete made them cut. Thirty-one of us. Old Sallow counted. He always counted.
@end

@scene N42_DIGGER
say survivor | I dig for the guild hall's bell. I don't need the bell. I need to have dug for it.
@end

@scene N42_SLUICE1
if flag:n42_sluice1 done
set n42_sluice1
sfx FX010
shake 0.4
say narr | You drag the lever down. Somewhere a gate slams, and the black current over the walkway sinks to a trickle.
end
label done
say narr | The lever is down. The gallery race runs low.
@end

@scene N42_SLUICE2
if flag:n42_sluice2 done
set n42_sluice2
sfx FX010
shake 0.5
say narr | The valve wheel fights you, then gives. Across the hall, the floodgate over the keeper's stair grinds open.
end
label done
say narr | The valve is open. The race hisses past below you.
@end

@scene N42_SHORT
if flag:n42_shortcut done
set n42_shortcut
sfx FX010
say narr | You draw the bolts on the maintenance hatch. Stale air, a ladder, and the dam foot far below.
end
label done
say narr | The hatch stands open onto the ladder down to the dam foot.
@end

@scene N42_DROWNED
say survivor | I came up to clear the grates. She told me to go home. I didn't listen. Now I clear them forever.
say survivor | She's not cruel. She's just holding a lot of water, and nobody ever said thank you.
@end

@scene N42_ORRIN
if flag:n42_cleared after
say orrin | Orrin. I kept this dam twenty years. The Crown wanted the water held for the furnaces. The valley wanted it let out slow.
say orrin | I drowned in the sluices trying to do both. My wife came up after me. She's been holding it ever since. Holding everything.
say orrin | When the gates shudder, she's about to let a breach go through her. Brace for it. And lightning - she never could abide storms.
say orrin | Don't hate her. She's the only reason Kettle Row is still dry.
end
label after
say orrin | She's resting. The dam isn't. Tell the guild to cut that road. Tell them Orrin said so. They never listened to Orrin.
@end

@scene N42_BOSS once
say narr | The woman on the crest walk is made of water held in the shape of a woman. Cracks run across her like veins.
say sluicewife | Go home. Go HOME. Every one of you comes up here to tell me it's safe, and then you go home and I hold it.
say sluicewife | Forty years. Letters. Certificates. Telescopes in the fog. Not one of you ever held a single drop of it.
say dain | We're not from the Crown. We came to help.
say sluicewife | Then HOLD IT.
say narr | Every sluice gate on the dam shudders at once.
battle BX11 noflee
set n42_cleared
say narr | The Sluicewife sinks to her knees. The water in her runs clear, then slows.
say sluicewife | ...I can let it out slow. Orrin always said slow. I was so angry I forgot.
say sluicewife | I can hold it a while longer, like this. Not forever. Tell them to cut the road. Tell them to go uphill.
say narr | She sinks into the crest walk and is part of the dam again. The weeping below grows quieter.
journal | The Weeping Dam: the Sluicewife is at rest and lets the water out slowly. She warned that the dam will not hold forever.
@end

# ================================================================ P01 Spine of Ilyrath (post-fault dungeon in the Scar, R01; boss BX12 Marrow Tyrant)
# Mechanism: memory crystals. Touching the fragment-crystals grown from the bone lets Ilyr remember, and the bone
# unlocks. Two on the Vertebral Walk open the way to the ribcage; the third opens the throat to the skull.
@scene P01_ENTER once
say narr | The fault split the plain and something rose out of it: a ridge of white bone a league long. Ribs arch over the road like the vaults of a cathedral.
say ilyr | ...
say dain | Ilyr?
say ilyr | I know this shape. I know it from the inside.
@end

@scene P01_PILGRIM
say survivor | Dragonborn pilgrims came as soon as it rose. We walk the spine and leave a scale at every rib. My grandmother said a great one lay under the plain.
say survivor | Something else got here first. It nests in the skull. It eats marrow and it's grown enormous on it. We hear it cracking the bones at night.
@end

@scene P01_NAME
say narr | Carved at the root of the rib, in the old dragonborn script, weathered almost smooth: ILYRATH.
say ilyr | That was my name when I had all of it. Before I was a fragment. Before I was a guest in your chest.
say dain | This is your body.
say ilyr | It was. I let go of it a long time ago, to keep the part that mattered. I did not think I would have to walk through it.
@end

@scene P01_MEM1
if flag:p01_mem1 done
set p01_mem1
sfx FX029
flash
say narr | You lay a hand on the crystal. It warms. For a moment you are flying - over the river farms, over a March with no roads, no Crown, no Veyr.
say ilyr | Before the builders. Before anyone built anything. There was just the wind, and the river, and me.
say narr | Deep in the spine, bone grinds against bone and loosens.
end
label done
say narr | The crystal is warm, and quiet.
@end

@scene P01_MEM2
if flag:p01_mem2 done
set p01_mem2
sfx FX029
flash
say narr | The crystal warms. You hear singing - small voices, in a language of hisses and clicks. Dragonborn children, on a hill, singing up at the sky.
say ilyr | They sang to me. I never answered. I thought there would always be time.
say narr | Somewhere ahead, the vertebrae unlock with a sound like a knuckle cracking.
end
label done
say narr | The crystal is warm. Far away, very faintly, the song goes on.
@end

@scene P01_MEM3
if flag:p01_mem3 done
set p01_mem3
sfx FX029
flash
shake 0.5
say narr | The last crystal burns cold. Iron hands close around your heart and PULL. A crown of chains, a ring of men in masks, and a vast body going limp.
say ilyr | They came for the heart. Men with seals and relays, and one of them wore a crown. I let go of the body to keep the heart out of their hands.
say ilyr | I have been small ever since. In a jar. In a seal. In a soldier. ...In you. You are the best of the three.
say dain | I'll take that.
say narr | The knot of bone across the ribcage's throat unravels.
end
label done
say narr | The crystal is cold. It has nothing left to remember.
@end

@scene P01_SHORT
if flag:p01_shortcut done
set p01_shortcut
sfx FX010
say narr | You heave the loose vertebrae out of the gap between the ribs. Daylight: the Rib Gate, just below.
end
label done
say narr | The gap between the ribs is clear.
@end

@scene P01_SCAV
say survivor | Marrow's worth a fortune since the fault. Burns like oil, heals like magic. I only take from the small bones.
say survivor | ...That's a lie. I take from anything I can reach. The big one in the skull takes more. We're both thieves. It's just bigger.
@end

@scene P01_ELDER
if flag:p01_cleared after
say elder | I came to pray at the skull. The Tyrant nests in the heart-cavity now. Every night it cracks another rib.
say elder | It's a scavenger. It eats what's already dead and calls it strength. When it breathes in the dust, hide your eyes. Storm breaks its hide.
end
label after
say elder | The heart-cavity is quiet. We'll leave scales there now, and sing. Maybe someone will hear it this time.
@end

@scene P01_ALTAR
say narr | Scales, teeth, and a child's clay dragon with a lopsided smile. Someone has written on the altar in chalk: 'COME BACK.'
if party:C01 raven
end
label raven
say ilyr | I cannot. But I can go on. Tell them that, if you ever find the singers.
@end

@scene P01_BOSS once
say narr | The heart-cavity is a nest of cracked bone and shed scales. Something huge lifts its head from a split thighbone, marrow dripping from its jaws.
say narr | It is a dragon, but a lesser one: all belly and teeth, fat on the dead.
say ilyr | Get out of my ribs.
say dain | He means it.
battle BX12 noflee
set p01_cleared
say narr | The Marrow Tyrant crashes down among the bones it stripped. Ilyrath's skull is silent.
say ilyr | ...Thank you. It is only bone. But it was mine. It is good to leave it clean.
journal | Spine of Ilyrath: the Marrow Tyrant is dead. The ridge is Ilyr's old body, and he has walked through it.
@end

@scene P01_HEART
say narr | The hollow where a dragon's heart once burned. The bone is scorched from the inside, as if the heart left in a hurry.
if flag:p01_cleared quiet
end
label quiet
say ilyr | It left to keep burning. It is still burning. Feel.
say narr | Under your breastbone, something warm beats twice, very gently.
@end

# ================================================================ P02 The Tessellate (post-fault builder tower, R01; boss BX13 The Tessellate Engine)
# Mechanism: schedule consoles. The tower lays a fresh course of masonry across its own corridors every dawn; each
# console pauses construction and the course retracts. A builder service lift is the shortcut back to the plaza.
# QC17 (Night Rider) "The Highway That Was", surface half: Mile Marker 0 (hook) -> three traffic logs -> the dispatch
# console after the Engine falls (patrol drones, decision) -> qc17_surface_done; the Deep half is at U22.
@scene P02_ENTER once
say narr | Where the plains were flat, a tower now stands, built of interlocking grey tiles that fit together too well. It is still growing.
say narr | Every few breaths, somewhere high above, a new tile slides into place with a soft, final click.
@end

@scene P02_SURVEYOR
say lanne | Surveyor Lanne, formerly of the Crown Survey. The tower rose the night of the fault. It adds one course of tiles every dawn.
say lanne | It's building something to a plan nobody alive has seen. I would very much like to see the plan. I would also like to not be demolished.
@end

@scene P02_CAMPER
say survivor | We camp in its shadow. It's warm at the base - the tiles hum. Nothing attacks the camp. I think the tower doesn't notice us.
@end

@scene P02_KID
say child | I found a road under the grass! A black one, smooth as glass, with white lines. It goes right under the tower. Mum says don't dig.
@end

@scene P02_COURSE1
if flag:p02_course1 done
set p02_course1
sfx FX010
say narr | The console flickers: CONSTRUCTION PAUSED - COURSE 4417. Down in the central shaft, the fresh masonry slides back into the walls.
end
label done
say narr | The console reads: PAUSED. It sounds offended.
@end

@scene P02_COURSE2
if flag:p02_course2 done
set p02_course2
sfx FX010
say narr | CONSTRUCTION PAUSED - COURSE 4418. The course below you folds away. The shaft runs clear down to the lower floor.
end
label done
say narr | The console reads: PAUSED.
@end

@scene P02_COURSE3
if flag:p02_course3 done
set p02_course3
sfx FX010
say narr | TRAFFIC CONTROL: LANE CLOSURE LIFTED. The stair course grinds back into the wall. The way up is open.
end
label done
say narr | LANE CLOSURE LIFTED. Somewhere a builder voice adds: DRIVE SAFELY.
@end

@scene P02_LIFT
if flag:p02_lift done
set p02_lift
sfx FX010
say narr | You release the lift chain. The service cage drops down the shaft to meet you, then waits, humming. It runs straight down to the plaza.
end
label done
say narr | The service lift hums, ready.
@end

@scene P02_CLIMBER
say worker | I climb the outside for the Survey. The tiles are warm and they move under your hands. Don't look at the joins too long. They look back.
say worker | Up top, the governor runs everything. It announces demolition before it does it. Very polite. Very thorough. Hit its core and it loses its place in the schedule.
@end

@scene P02_BOSS once
say narr | The crown of the tower is a single vast room. At its centre, something of grey tiles and white light unfolds from the floor and stands.
say tessellate | VISITORS. NOT ON SCHEDULE. CONSTRUCTION WILL CONTINUE AROUND YOU.
say dain | What are you building?
say tessellate | THE PLAN. THE PLAN IS 4418 COURSES COMPLETE OF 90112. ESTIMATED COMPLETION: 212 YEARS. DEMOLITION OF OBSTRUCTIONS: NOW.
battle BX13 noflee
set p02_cleared
say narr | The Engine's light dims. Tile by tile it lays itself down on the floor and stops.
say tessellate | SCHEDULE... SUSPENDED. PLAN... ARCHIVED.
say narr | Far below, for the first time since the fault, no new tile clicks into place at dawn.
journal | The Tessellate: the tower's governor is stopped. The tower no longer grows.
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- QC17 The Highway That Was (surface half)
@scene QC17_MARKER
if recruited:C17 rider
say narr | A builder road sign, bent double, half buried: MILE 0. RING HIGHWAY - ALL LANES.
end
label rider
if flag:qc17_hook known
set qc17_hook
say rider | Mile zero. I haven't seen this sign since - since the last night the lights were on.
say rider | This was my road. The Ring Highway. Six lanes round the whole plain. The tower's sitting right on the interchange.
say dain | You patrolled it.
say rider | I still patrol it. Nobody ever told me the patrol was over. There's a traffic control deck in there. If anyone ever wrote down why the road shut, it's there.
quest QC17 ACTIVE marker
journal | QC17 The Highway That Was: the Tessellate stands on Mile 0 of the Night Rider's old highway. Its traffic control deck may say why the road was shut.
objective rider | Search the Tessellate's traffic control deck for the highway's last logs.
end
label known
say rider | Mile zero. Everything I am starts here.
@end

@scene QC17_LOG1
if q:QC17:ACTIVE quest
say narr | A builder console, dead. Lines of old light are burned into the glass: LANE 1 - VOLUME NOMINAL.
end
label quest
if flag:qc17_log1 read
set qc17_log1
say narr | The console wakes at the Rider's touch. LANE 1, FINAL NIGHT: VOLUME ZERO. ALL UNITS HOLD POSITION PENDING RELIEF.
say rider | Volume zero. There was nobody on the road. They pulled everybody off, and they told the patrol to stay.
call QC17_CHECK
end
label read
say rider | Hold position pending relief. That's the last thing anyone told me.
@end

@scene QC17_LOG2
if q:QC17:ACTIVE quest
say narr | A dead console: LANE 6 - SURFACE DECK SEALED.
end
label quest
if flag:qc17_log2 read
set qc17_log2
say narr | LANE 6, FINAL NIGHT: SURFACE DECK SEALED. TRAFFIC DIVERTED TO LOWER DECK - LATTICE CONNECTOR.
say rider | Lower deck. The highway had a lower deck? They sent everything underground and left the surface to us.
call QC17_CHECK
end
label read
say rider | Diverted underground. To the Lattice. That's where everyone went.
@end

@scene QC17_LOG3
if q:QC17:ACTIVE quest
say narr | The dispatch relay's single key glows, waiting for a patrol code.
end
label quest
if flag:qc17_log3 read
set qc17_log3
say narr | The Rider types a code from memory. The relay answers: PATROL UNIT 17 - STATUS ACTIVE - RELIEF: NOT SCHEDULED.
say rider | Not scheduled. They never meant to send anyone. They just forgot to tell us to go home.
say rider | The master dispatch console will be at the top, with the governor. If I can reach it, I can close my own patrol. Or not.
call QC17_CHECK
end
label read
say rider | Relief not scheduled. The top of the tower. That's where I end this, one way or another.
@end

@scene QC17_CHECK
if flag:qc17_log1,flag:qc17_log2,flag:qc17_log3 ready
end
label ready
quest QC17 ACTIVE logs
objective rider | Reach the master dispatch console at the crown of the Tessellate.
@end

@scene QC17_CONSOLE
if flag:qc17_surface_done done
if qs:QC17:logs rider
say narr | The master dispatch console of the Ring Highway. A single line blinks: PATROL UNITS AWAITING DISPATCH.
end
label rider
if !flag:p02_cleared locked
say narr | The Rider lays his hand on the console. Every screen in the room wakes. PATROL UNIT 17 RECOGNISED.
say rider | There. Dispatch. I can close the patrol from here.
say narr | Panels in the walls slide open. Patrol drones - squat, armoured, rimmed with red light - roll out and turn toward him.
say tessellate | UNAUTHORISED PATROL CLOSURE. UNITS: ENFORCE SCHEDULE.
battle OWP_2
say narr | The last drone rolls to a stop at the Rider's boots. Its red light goes out.
say rider | They were on patrol too. Same orders. Same night. Nobody came for them either.
say narr | The console waits. Two commands glow: PATROL ENDED - UNIT RELIEVED, and PATROL CONTINUES - NEW ROUTE.
choice | Tell him to end it. He's earned rest -> ended | Tell him to choose a new road -> route
label ended
set qc17_ended
say dain | You held it long enough. Close it. You're relieved.
say rider | ...PATROL ENDED. UNIT RELIEVED.
say narr | The console clicks. For a moment the Rider sits very still, like a man who has put down something heavy he forgot he was carrying.
say rider | Strange. I don't feel lighter. I feel like I have room. I don't know what to put in it yet.
goto after
label route
set qc17_route
say dain | The roads out there are broken and there's nobody watching them. Pick a new route.
say rider | ...PATROL CONTINUES. NEW ROUTE: WHEREVER THEY'RE WALKING.
say narr | The console accepts the order without complaint, as if it had been waiting for someone to give it.
say rider | The causeways. The Refuge Rock stair. The Oathstone road. I'll ride them all. Somebody should.
label after
say narr | One more line scrolls up the screen: LOWER DECK - LATTICE CONNECTOR - STATUS: LIGHTS ON.
say rider | Lights on. Down there, under the plain. The rest of my road is still lit, and something's driving on it.
set qc17_surface_done
quest QC17 ACTIVE deep
give A111 1
journal | QC17 The Highway That Was: the Rider has settled his patrol at the Tessellate. The highway's lower deck runs on into the Lattice - the Highway That Was, deep below - and its lights are on.
objective rider | Find the Ring Highway's lower deck in the Lattice: the Highway That Was.
end
label locked
say narr | The master dispatch console, dark behind the governor's cradle. The governor's light holds every screen in the room.
end
label done
say narr | The console reads: LOWER DECK - LATTICE CONNECTOR - LIGHTS ON.
@end

# ================================================================ P05 Refuge Rock (post-fault village, R03) + QW1 "Refuge Rock"
# QW1 beats: hook (Marit) -> Tide Shelf: Ansa (crab fight), Dov (splint), the wreck (Tamm, Wynn and Sergeant Kell's
# deserters) -> stage plead -> harpy ambush on the way home (qw1_home) -> council decision at Marit -> COMPLETED.
@scene QW1_MARIT
if q:QW1 done
if qs:QW1:plead plead
if q:QW1:ACTIVE active
say marit | Marit Oakes. Warden of the Rock, because somebody had to be and I was standing nearest the rope.
say marit | Two hundred people on a rock that used to be a cormorant colony. We have boats, tents and one spring. The spring is failing.
say marit | And four of my fishers went down to the Tide Shelf three days ago, to salvage the wreck there. Tamm, Ansa, Dov. And Wynn - she's eleven. She wasn't supposed to go.
say marit | The gulls have been circling the shelf since. Gulls don't circle for fish.
choice | We'll bring them back -> take | Tell me about the wreck -> wreck
label wreck
say marit | A Crown supply hulk, washed up in the fault. If there's food or water gear aboard, it could keep us through winter. If there's Crown aboard, it could finish us.
label take
say dain | We'll go down. Open the shelf gate.
say marit | Untie it yourself, the knot's mine. Bring them back. All of them, or tell me which ones to mourn.
set qw1_started
quest QW1 ACTIVE shelf
journal | QW1 Refuge Rock: four fishers are missing on the Tide Shelf below the Rock. Bring them back to Warden Marit.
objective marit | Find the missing fishers on the Tide Shelf.
end
label active
say marit | The shelf stair's south-east. Four of them. Ansa, Dov, Tamm, and Wynn.
end
label plead
if flag:qw1_home decide
say marit | Get them up the stair first. We'll talk in daylight.
end
label decide
call QW1_DECISION
end
label done
if flag:qw1_kell_in in
say marit | Kell's lot are camped on the shelf with half the rations. They send water up every morning, and nobody's said thank you yet. We will.
end
label in
say marit | The still drips all day now. Kell's lot take turns on it. Old Fenn still won't sit next to them at supper. He will.
@end

@scene QW1_DECISION once
say marit | Ansa. Dov. Tamm. Wynn. All four of them home, and Wynn with a scolding coming she'll remember when she's ninety.
say marit | Now. The other thing. Tamm says there are Crown soldiers on that wreck. Nine of them, and a sergeant.
say tamm | They kept us alive, Warden. Harpies came every night. The sergeant stood in the hatch with a boat-hook.
say kell | Sergeant Kell, late of the Gallowgate Toll. We walked away from the Crown when the ground broke. We've a salvaged water still and the hulk's rations.
say kell | We'll give you both either way. But we'd like to come up. We're tired of the shelf. We're tired of being the Crown.
say marit | Half this rock lost somebody to a Crown toll, a Crown levy, a Crown convoy. You're asking me to feed their collectors.
say marit | ...You brought my people home. You decide. I'll stand behind it, and so will the council, or they can find their own rock.
choice | Let the deserters in -> in | Keep them on the shelf, with a share of what they brought -> out
label in
set qw1_kell_in
say dain | They held a hatch against harpies for strangers. That's the only oath worth anything now. Let them in.
say marit | ...Then they're in. They sleep on the east side, they carry water, and they learn every name on the council table.
say kell | We'll learn them. I used to collect names. I'd like to hand some back.
goto reward
label out
set qw1_kell_out
say dain | Not yet. Give the Rock time. Let them camp on the shelf, share the rations, run the still, and earn it.
say kell | Fair. It's more than the Crown ever gave anyone. We'll send water up every morning. Leave the gate unknotted.
say marit | The gate stays open. The door can come later. Maybe that's how it should be.
label reward
quest QW1 RESOLUTION_READY council
say marit | The still will save the spring. The rations will save the winter. You saved four people I'd already started to mourn.
say marit | Take this. It was the lighthouse keeper's charm. He said it kept the lamp honest.
give A117 1
give I003 2
gold 2000
quest QW1 COMPLETED done
journal | QW1 Refuge Rock: the fishers are home, the salvaged still feeds the spring, and Sergeant Kell's deserters have their answer.
@end

@scene QW1_WAITING
if flag:qw1_home home
say baker | My Tamm went down to the shelf with the others. I stand here because from here you can see the top of the stair.
say baker | If you're going down, tell him the baby's teeth came in. He'll want to know. He'll want to come up and see.
end
label home
say baker | He's home. He's asleep, the lump. The baby's biting him. I've never seen him so happy.
@end

@scene QW1_CRABS
say narr | Out on the tide pools a woman stands on a rock, swinging a gaff at a ring of snapping, barnacled shells.
say ansa | About time! Don't just stand there, they're HUNGRY!
battle POWX_R03_1
set qw1_ansa
say ansa | Ansa. Thank you. They had me on that rock for a day. The others are in the wreck, with - well. You'll see.
say ansa | Dov went up the north rocks for a better look and didn't come down. I think his leg's gone.
say ansa | I'll wait here for the others. I'm not walking home alone after that.
call QW1_CHECK
@end

@scene QW1_ANSA
if flag:qw1_ansa done
say ansa | Watch the pools!
end
label done
say ansa | I'll wait for the others. Dov's up the north rocks. The rest are in the wreck.
@end

@scene QW1_DOV
if flag:qw1_dov done
set qw1_dov
say dov | Don't laugh. I climbed up for a look and the rock climbed down without me. The leg's broken.
say narr | You splint the leg with a boat-slat and a strip of sail. Dov swears in three coastal dialects and one Crown one.
say dov | Tamm and the girl are in the hulk with the soldiers. The soldiers are - I don't know what they are. They didn't hurt us.
call QW1_CHECK
end
label done
say dov | I'll hop. I'm a very good hopper. Don't wait for me.
@end

@scene QW1_WRECK
if flag:qw1_wreck done
set qw1_wreck
say narr | Inside the hulk, lamplight. Two fishers - a man and a girl - sit by a patched Crown still, drinking from tin cups. Crown soldiers sit with them.
say kell | Don't draw. We're not what we look like. Or we are, and we've stopped.
say dain | Sergeant Kell. Gallowgate Toll.
say kell | You've a memory. I'm done collecting names, sir. We found these two with the harpies on them and brought them in.
say tamm | It's true. They've been sharing the rations. The girl sleeps behind the sergeant. Nobody gets near her.
say kell | Take them home. And - ask your Warden. If there's room on that rock for nine soldiers who'd rather not be.
quest QW1 ACTIVE wreck
call QW1_CHECK
end
label done
say kell | Take your fishers up. We'll follow when your Warden says, or we won't. Either way, the still goes to the Rock.
@end

@scene QW1_CHECK
if flag:qw1_ansa,flag:qw1_dov,flag:qw1_wreck ready
end
label ready
quest QW1 ACTIVE plead
objective marit | Take the fishers home up the shelf stair, then speak to Warden Marit about Kell's deserters.
@end

@scene QW1_WYNN
say child | I'm Wynn. I'm not in trouble. I came to help. I helped. The sergeant says I'm a very good lookout.
@end

@scene QW1_SOLDIER
say guard | I was a toll guard. Now I'm a harpy guard. The pay's worse and I've never been prouder.
@end

@scene QW1_AMBUSH once
say narr | Halfway to the stair, the gulls stop circling. They fold their wings and drop. They are not gulls.
say kell | Harpies! Fishers to the rocks - soldiers, form on me!
battle POWX_R03_3
set qw1_home
say narr | The harpies break and scatter out to sea. Everyone climbs the stair together: fishers, soldiers, and a very proud lookout.
@end

@scene QW1_ANSA_HOME
say ansa | Back on the nets. I'll never complain about mending again. That's a lie. But I'll mean it for a week.
@end

@scene QW1_DOV_HOME
say dov | Leg's mending. Marit says I can't climb anything taller than a chair until spring. I climbed the chair. She saw.
@end

@scene QW1_TAMM_HOME
if flag:qw1_kell_in in
say tamm | Kell's lot camp down on the shelf. I take the baby down to see them. The sergeant holds her like she's made of glass.
end
label in
say tamm | The soldiers sleep on the east side. They carry water up the stair twice a day. Nobody asked them to do it twice.
@end

@scene QW1_WYNN_HOME
say child | I'm grounded until I'm twelve. That's in four months. I'm counting.
@end

@scene QW1_KELL_CAMP
say kell | We run the still and send the water up at dawn. The gate's unknotted. One day somebody will come down and ask us to supper.
@end

@scene QW1_KELL_HOME
say kell | Kell. Just Kell. I carry water and I learn names off the council table. Forty-one so far. They're better names than the ones I used to collect.
@end

@scene QW1_DESERTER
say guard | They gave me a tent and a job. Nobody's asked for my papers. I keep waiting for someone to ask for my papers.
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Refuge Rock townsfolk
@scene P05_CISTERN
if q:QW1 fixed
say narr | The cistern is cracked and barely wet. A tally of water rations is chalked on the rim, each line smaller than the last.
end
label fixed
say narr | A patched Crown still drips into the cistern day and night. Someone has chalked over the ration tally: ENOUGH.
@end

@scene P05_BOATWRIGHT
say sailor | I build boats out of other boats. The fault broke a whole fleet on the shoals. I'm never short of parts. I'm always short of hands.
@end

@scene P05_NETMENDER
say baker | Every net here is three nets knotted together. You can tell whose by the knots. Glass Coast knots are neat. Veyr knots are angry.
@end

@scene P05_LOOKOUT
say guard | I watch the sea for boats, harpies and the Crown. So far: boats. Harpies. No Crown. I'm grateful.
say guard | North-east, past the cliffs, there's a signal tower on a headland. Dark. If anyone lit it, you could see it from half the ruin.
rumor | Refuge Rock: a dark signal tower stands on a headland to the north-east. Lit, it would be seen across half the ruin.
@end

@scene P05_MOTHER
say baker | I walked out of Veyr carrying my daughter and a bag of the wrong things. A clock. Two left shoes. I don't know why.
say baker | The Rock took us anyway. The clock still works. It's the only one here. People come to ask me the time.
@end

@scene P05_KID
say child | I found a crab with a Crown seal stuck on its shell! I named it Sergeant. It pinched the real sergeant. Everybody laughed.
@end

@scene P05_KID2
say child | Marit says we'll have a school when we have a roof. We have a roof! It's upside down. It still counts.
@end

@scene P05_WEND
say wend | Refuge Rock! Worst harbour, best customers. Nobody here has any money, so I take payment in news and fish.
say wend | Tell them at the Oathstone I'm alive. They worry. Well. Some of them owe me, so they worry.
shop SHOP_WEND
@end

@scene P05_SCRIBE
say clerk | I was a clerk for the Ministry of Provision. I used to write down what the Crown took. Now I write down who arrives.
say clerk | Two hundred and twelve. I write the name, where they walked from, and one thing they want. Most want the same thing. To stop walking.
@end

@scene P05_SPRING
if q:QW1 fixed
say worker | Spring-keeper. The fault cracked the rock and the spring's leaking into the sea. I'm rationing a cup a head. Soon half a cup.
end
label fixed
say worker | The still makes more water than the spring ever did. I'm a still-keeper now. It's a promotion. I've asked for a hat.
@end

@scene P05_DRAGONBORN
say survivor | I'm dragonborn. In Veyr they'd have numbered me for the relays. Here, Marit put me on the net crew and asked my name.
say survivor | Your Raven carries a dragon, they say. Tell him - tell him there are more of us walking out of the ruin than anyone thinks.
@end

@scene P05_LIBRARIAN
say librarian | I was a tide-librarian at Bellharbor. I saved one crate of books from the flood. It turned out to be the cookery section.
say librarian | So the Rock eats well, at least. We are working through the soups alphabetically.
@end

@scene P05_INN
say keeper | The Upturned Hull. Bunks, blankets, and a stove made from a ship's boiler. Pay what you can. You can, can't you?
inn 20
@end

@scene P05_SLEEPER
say survivor | Please. I walked from the Veyr Rift. I've slept four hours since the fault. Let me make it five.
@end

@scene P05_CARDS
say sailor | We play for fish. I'm up three mackerel and a very small eel. The eel's a promissory eel. I haven't caught it yet.
@end

@scene P05_SHOPKEEP
say keeper | Salvager Quill. Whatever the sea brings up, I sell. Mostly Crown gear, lightly drowned. Works fine once it's dried.
shop SHOP_P05
@end

@scene P05_CRAFTER
if flag:p05_nan met
set p05_nan
say nan | Nan. Wreckwright. I make things out of wrecks, which is lucky, because that's all the world is right now.
say nan | Bring me salvage - brass, oak, sailcloth, Crown steel - and whatever you've pulled off the beasts out there. I'll make it into something that floats. Or stabs.
end
label met
say nan | Salvage brass, drowned oak, sailcloth, beast hide, Crown steel. The ruin is full of materials. It's just badly arranged.
@end

@scene P05_COUNCIL
if flag:qw1_kell_in in
say elder | Councillor Fenn. I vote on everything. Mostly 'no'. Somebody has to test the ideas.
end
label in
say elder | Crown soldiers on the Rock. I voted no. I was outvoted. The sergeant brought me tea. I'm still voting no. Slower.
@end

@scene P05_COUNCIL2
say baker | Councillor Rue. I run the kitchens, so I run the council. Nobody argues with the person who ladles.
@end

@scene P05_HALLSCRIBE
say clerk | Every name that arrives gets carved into the table edge. We're running out of edge. Rue wants a second table.
@end

# ================================================================ P09 The Last Beacon (post-fault landmark, R04) + QW6 "The Last Beacon"
# QW6 beats: hook (Hesk) -> the dead keeper's cairn on the cliff ledge (key) -> lamp gallery: the griffon roost (climb)
# -> the fire-cup decision (heartglass or the camp's winter oil) -> the beacon burns: fast-travel network, and the
# Lanternwake's Grapnel Keel (flag lanternwake_grapnel).
@scene QW6_HESK
if q:QW6 done
if qs:QW6:lamp lamp
if qs:QW6:climb climb
if q:QW6:ACTIVE active
say hesk | Hesk, Signal Company of the Skyspine. What's left of it: me, two keepers, a child, and a tower with no light in it.
say hesk | The Last Beacon was the master lamp. When it burned, every waystone and signal lantern in sight answered. Roads by night. Ships home.
say hesk | The fault put it out. The keeper, Old Mattock, went up to relight it and came down the cliff instead of the stair. He had the key.
say hesk | He's on the ledge below the west cliff. We built him a cairn. We couldn't bring ourselves to go through his pockets.
choice | We'll relight it -> take | What's in it for the ruin? -> why
label why
say hesk | Light it, and the beacon wakes the stones - the old keepers' network. You'd walk from one lit stone to the next.
say hesk | And your airship. The Company kept plans for a grapnel keel, for landing where there's no field. Light the lamp and my crew will fit it.
label take
say dain | We'll get the key.
say hesk | Ladder's down past the camp, at the west cliff. Be gentle with him. He was always cold.
quest QW6 ACTIVE key
journal | QW6 The Last Beacon: relight the dark signal tower. The keeper's key is with his body, on the ledge below the west cliff.
objective hesk | Find Old Mattock's key on the cliff ledge below the Last Beacon.
end
label active
say hesk | The ladder's on the west side, past the tents. His cairn has a lantern on it. We couldn't light that either.
end
label climb
say hesk | You've the key. The stair goes up to the lamp gallery. Griffons have been roosting up there since the fault. They won't like company.
end
label lamp
say hesk | The fire-cup's empty, isn't it. It always was the hard part.
end
label done
say hesk | Every night I climb up and look. Stones answering on the islands, lanterns on the causeways. Like the ruin remembering it's a country.
@end

@scene QW6_CAIRN
if flag:qw6_key done
if q:QW6:ACTIVE take
say narr | A cairn with a dark lantern on top. Beneath it, an old man in a signal keeper's coat, hands folded over his chest.
end
label take
set qw6_key
say narr | Old Mattock's hands are folded over a bronze key shaped like a lantern. It comes away easily, as if he'd been holding it out.
say narr | There's a stub of candle in his coat pocket. You light the cairn lantern with it before you go.
quest QW6 ACTIVE climb
objective hesk | Climb the Last Beacon to the lamp gallery.
end
label done
say narr | The cairn lantern burns, small and steady, over Old Mattock.
@end

@scene QW6_ROOST once
say narr | The gallery is thick with feathers and signal-flag nests. Something huge shifts behind the great lamp and unfolds its wings.
battle POWX_R04_4
quest QW6 ACTIVE lamp
say narr | The griffons break out through the gallery windows and wheel away over the sea. The great lamp stands in the silence.
@end

@scene QW6_LAMP
if q:QW6 lit
if qs:QW6:lamp decide
say narr | A lens of a thousand prisms around an empty fire-cup. It is dark and very cold.
end
label decide
say narr | The fire-cup is empty. In the lamp housing, wrapped in Crown oilcloth, is a shard of red crystal as long as your forearm. Heartglass.
say narr | A note in the keepers' hand is tied to it: 'SENT BY THE MINISTRY. BURNS A HUNDRED YEARS. WE DID NOT ASK WHERE IT CAME FROM.'
say ilyr | We know where it came from.
say dain | Dragonborn. Ground down in the relays until they were fuel.
say narr | Below, in the camp, Hesk's keepers are breaking up their last cask of winter lamp-oil, just in case.
choice | Burn the heartglass - it's already been paid for -> glass | Burn the camp's oil, and bury the heartglass -> oil
label glass
set qw6_heartglass
say dain | It's already been paid for. Nobody gets it back by leaving it in a box. Let it light somebody home.
say ilyr | ...Then let it burn for them. All of them. I'll remember whose it was.
say narr | The heartglass catches with a sound like a held breath let go. Red light floods the lens and pours out over the sea.
goto lit_now
label oil
set qw6_oil
say dain | No. The Crown burned enough of them. We'll burn oil, and we'll carry more.
say narr | You carry the heartglass down, and the keepers carry the oil up. It burns lower and yellower than a Crown lamp. It burns.
say narr | Old Mattock's ledge gets a second stone that night: the heartglass, buried under the cairn, with its note.
label lit_now
set p09_beacon_lit
sfx FX029
flash
say narr | Far out on the ruin, one by one, the waystones answer. Lanterns on the causeways. A lamp on Refuge Rock. A light on the Oathstone.
call QW6_REWARD
end
label lit
say narr | The great lamp burns. Every prism throws a road across the dark.
@end

@scene QW6_REWARD once
say hesk | Look at it. LOOK at it. Mattock, you old goat, look at it.
if flag:qw6_oil oil
say hesk | Red light. It's beautiful. I'll hate it every night, and I'll climb up every night to look at it.
goto keel
label oil
say hesk | Oil. We'll be cold this winter. We'll be cold and we'll be proud of it. Tobiah Wend owes me a favour. He's about to find out.
label keel
say hesk | Now: your airship. The Company kept plans for a grapnel keel - hooks and a winch, so a ship can moor on any cliff or field.
say hesk | My crew will have it fitted by morning. Land where you like. The ruin's yours to walk again.
set lanternwake_grapnel
say narr | (The Lanternwake is fitted with the Grapnel Keel: she can now land anywhere on open ground.)
say narr | (The Last Beacon wakes the keepers' network: lit waystones can be used to travel between them.)
discover L_P09
quest QW6 COMPLETED lit
give A118 1
gold 3000
journal | QW6 The Last Beacon: the beacon burns again, the waystone network is awake, and the Lanternwake carries the Grapnel Keel.
@end

@scene P09_KEEPER1
if flag:p09_beacon_lit lit
say survivor | Signal-keeper, second class. There's no first class anymore. There's no anything anymore, except this headland.
say survivor | We send flags by day. Nobody answers. You keep sending. That's what signal work is.
end
label lit
say survivor | Seventeen answering lights last night. Seventeen! I've written them all down. I'm going to write to every one of them.
@end

@scene P09_KEEPER2
if flag:p09_beacon_lit lit
say worker | Griffons took the gallery after the fault. They like the height and the flags. Can't blame them. Can't climb past them either.
end
label lit
say worker | We take turns in the gallery now, tending the lamp. It's warm up there. The griffons come back and sit on the rail, like they're guarding it.
@end

@scene P09_CHILD
say child | I'm the Signal Company's newest recruit. I know seven flags. This one means 'help'. This one means 'we're fine'. They look the same.
@end

@scene P09_LIFTMAN
say elder | The cable lift ran down to the harbour. The harbour's under the sea now. So it's a lift to nowhere. I still oil it.
@end

@scene P09_WEND
say wend | I saw the light from three islands away and turned my boat round. Hesk says I owe her oil. I don't remember owing her oil.
say wend | ...I'll bring the oil.
shop SHOP_WEND
@end

